-->AOP (Aspect Oriented Programming) is a programming technique used to separate common functionalities like 
   logging, security, transactions, and exception handling from business logic.
-->AOP helps us keep business code separate from extra work like:
   Logging
   Security
   Auditing
   Performance Monitoring
Real-Life Example : Imagine a student entering an exam hall.
Main task : Write Exam
Extra tasks : Check Hall Ticket, Check ID Card and Mark Attendance
--> These extra tasks happen before the main task.
--> AOP handles such extra tasks automatically.

Aspect : An Aspect is a class that contains cross-cutting concerns.
--> In Simple Words An Aspect is a special class where we write common code.

Advice : Advice is the action performed by an Aspect at a specific point in the program.
--> Advice is the actual code that runs.

Join Point : A Join Point is a point in the application where an Aspect can be applied.
--> Join Point is a method where Spring can execute additional code.
Example :
public void login() {}
public void register() {}
public void transferMoney() {}
--> All these methods are Join Points.

Pointcut : A Pointcut is an expression that selects specific Join Points.
Real-Life Example : Imagine a company has 100 rooms, 
                    The security guard is told to Check only rooms 10 to 20.
                    That instruction is the Pointcut.

Relationship Between Aspect, Advice, Join Point, Pointcut :

Aspect
   ↓
Contains
   ↓
Advice
   ↓
Runs At
   ↓
Join Point
   ↓
Selected By
   ↓
Pointcut

Types of Advice : 

1. Before Advice : Runs before a method executes.
2. After Advice : Runs after method execution.
3. After Returning Advice : Runs only if method completes successfully.
4. After Throwing Advice : Runs when an exception occurs.
5. Around Advice : Runs before and after the method.

Lombok : 
Lombok is a Java library that automatically generates boilerplate code such as
getters, setters, constructors, and toString() methods using annotations.

Common Lombok Annotations : 

@Getter : Creates getters.
@Setter : Creates setters.
@NoArgsConstructor : Creates a default constructor.
@AllArgsConstructor : Creates a constructor with all fields.
@Data : Most commonly used.
        Creates : Getters, Setters, toString(), equals() & hashCode()
--> @Data is a Lombok annotation that automatically generates commonly used methods such as 
    getters, setters, toString(), equals(), hashCode(), and a required-arguments constructor for a class.
--> While writing the code import lombok.annotations; for each annotation.




